{
  "analysis": "The review describes a chance-based wheel reward mechanic in the Genie game. After the wheel appears to stop on a valuable prize, the game allegedly moves it to a spin outcome and replaces the original reward with a lower one. This is both a variable spin-wheel reward system and an asserted manipulation of the outcome. The reviewer does not describe real-money spending or a paid stake, so Gambling and Intermediate Currency are not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the wheel spin after stopping on a great price the genie move the wheel to a spin tag, and then the prices after the spin are way lower than the original price",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging: where the span asserts that the game manipulates the determination of outcomes or tunes the result on a hidden schedule, assign Illusion of Control.",
      "rationale": "The span alleges that the game changes the wheel's result after it has stopped on a better prize, indicating manipulated rather than genuinely determined outcomes."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "the wheel spin after stopping on a great price",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards; Gacha mechanics like pulls, rolls, banners: chance-based spin-wheel delivery of rewards assigns Reward Mania.",
      "rationale": "The wheel spin is a chance-based mechanism delivering variable prize amounts."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review is primarily a technical-fault report: crashes prevent opening gifts or trading, lag prevents throwing Poké Balls and responding in battles, and freezing lets the opponent continue attacking. Under R11, these malfunction-attributed effects do not support dark-pattern labels such as forced continuation or manipulation. However, the review explicitly identifies two underlying game mechanics unrelated to the fault: trading with friends, which is a reciprocal social resource exchange, and PvP battles involving an opponent, which is player competition. No web search was needed.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "trade with friends",
      "rule_applied": "Indicator: the span names a social resource exchange or trade with other players.",
      "rationale": "Trading with friends is an explicit reciprocal exchange mechanic, even though the review says crashes prevent it from working."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the pvp & rockets battles",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: the game makes players compete against other players.",
      "rationale": "The explicit reference to PvP battles identifies competition between players; the reported lag is a separate technical fault."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a 30-second matchmaking delay, but this is a connection or queueing delay rather than an in-game timer that forces the player to follow a schedule, so it does not support Playing by Appointment. The statement that rewards are 'just for show' criticizes their apparent value or presentation, but it does not describe a complex, random, or otherwise manipulative reward-delivery system. The clearest design behavior is that obtaining weapon upgrades or perks requires continuing to battle and win, which describes repeated combat for progress and also places the player in competitive matches.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "Its difficult to get weapon upgrades or any percs others you stick battling to win",
      "rule_applied": "Definition; vs Slow Progress: the span identifies the repeated activity required for progress rather than merely saying progress is slow.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer implies that players must keep battling and winning to obtain weapon upgrades or perks."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "stick battling to win",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: the game makes players compete in battles or matches.",
      "rationale": "Battling to win describes competitive gameplay against an opposing side."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes competitive match play against opposing decks built around Mega Knight, P.E.K.K.A., and Golem, so it explicitly portrays players competing against one another. It also directly describes a pay-to-win advantage: the reviewer says advancement and winning increasingly depend on payment rather than skill. The complaints that these cards are annoying and that updates buff hated cards are balance evaluations, not Power Creep because no previously purchased item is said to lose value. There is also no clear claim that outcomes are secretly rigged or that harmful conduct is rewarded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "all you play against after 8k trophies is mega knight,pekka, and golem",
      "rule_applied": "definition: The game makes players compete against other players.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes facing opposing decks in competitive match play."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "its also pay to win",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator: Patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money.",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly states that spending money provides an advantage for winning and progressing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a move-limited condition involving 9 diamonds, but it does not clearly identify a timer, a chance-based reward, a real-money currency conversion, or a purchase-for-progress mechanic, so that passage does not support another label. “you don't have to pay to win” is a denial rather than a described monetization mechanic. “beyond the pass” refers to Fishdom’s purchasable seasonal Gold Pass; the search confirms that the pass grants benefits for one event and expires when that event ends, fitting the battle-pass indicator for Recurring Fee. ([playrix.helpshift.com](https://playrix.helpshift.com/hc/en/4-fishdom/faq/4134-season-challenges-1757580206/?p=web&utm_source=openai)) “trade lives” explicitly describes exchanging in-game resources with other players, fitting Reciprocity.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_RecurringFee",
      "span": "beyond the pass",
      "rule_applied": "Battle passes, MMO-subscriptions [indicators]; the search established that Fishdom’s pass is a purchasable seasonal Gold Pass that operates until the event ends. ([playrix.helpshift.com](https://playrix.helpshift.com/hc/en/4-fishdom/faq/4134-season-challenges-1757580206/?p=web&utm_source=openai))",
      "rationale": "The reviewer identifies a seasonal paid pass as an in-game purchase, which matches the battle-pass form of Recurring Fee."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "Get friends and a team and trade lives.",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: feeling obligated to return a favor, share resources, trade an item, etc.",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes trading lives with other players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "Fishdom pass battle pass paid pass",
  "search_result": "The official Fishdom Help Center identifies a purchasable Gold Pass for recurring seasonal events; the pass provides benefits until the event ends and then ceases to function. ([playrix.helpshift.com](https://playrix.helpshift.com/hc/en/4-fishdom/faq/4134-season-challenges-1757580206/?p=web&utm_source=openai))"
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two active time-based mechanics. First, lives regenerate on an in-game timer, and the reviewer suggests that a new life should arrive within 5–10 minutes; this implies a wait-to-play mechanic. Second, daily prizes reset when the player misses a day, which is a daily-return incentive with a missed-day penalty. The comments about harder levels, addiction, and unnecessary tasks do not by themselves establish grinding or another coded mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "maybe only 5-10 minutes for a new life",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that make players wait, including lives or energy refilling over time.",
      "rationale": "The span implies that lives are replenished after a real-time delay, restricting when the player can continue playing."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "just because you miss a day doesnt mean that you need to start over on your daily prizes",
      "rule_applied": "Daily Rewards: daily login incentives with an active consequence for missing a day, such as resetting the reward sequence.",
      "rationale": "Missing a day causes the daily-prize progression to start over."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game advertisement that cannot be dismissed normally: there is no close control, tapping the ad redirects to a store, returning to the game leaves the ad screen stuck, and the player must terminate the app to exit. The forced, effectively unskippable ad supports Advertisement. The absent close control and redirection of arbitrary taps to the store obstruct the opt-out and steer the player toward a purchase-related destination, supporting UI Misdirection. This is presented as a functioning interface behavior, not as a bug or technical fault, and there is no described completed purchase to support Easy to Purchase.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "You have to kill the game to come out of the ad screen",
      "rule_applied": "Forced to watch ads or given rewards for watching ads",
      "rationale": "The player cannot dismiss the in-game advertisement normally and must terminate the game to escape it."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "No cross mark on the screen , if you try to click anywhere on the screen it takes you to store.",
      "rule_applied": "UI Misdirection boundary rule: an interface that obstructs an opt-out by omitting a close control and routing taps toward a purchase-related destination",
      "rationale": "The missing close control and forced store redirection make opting out difficult while steering the player's attention and action toward the store."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a current consequence of leaving: deleting the game would cause the player to lose purchased items and accumulated progress, which makes abandoning the game costly. This matches invested or endowed value under Easy to Get, Hard to Lose. The request to add persistent saving is hypothetical and does not create a separate label, and the stated intention to spend money in the future does not show pay-to-progress.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "if I deleted the game I would lose everything my in-game purchases and my hard work I really don't want that to happen",
      "rule_applied": "Invested (Endowed) Value: having invested time and money into advancing one's status makes it challenging to abandon those gains.",
      "rationale": "The player is reluctant to delete the game because leaving would forfeit purchased value and accumulated progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review praises the game's graphics, storyline, strategy, empire-building, and real-time battles. The only coded mechanic is direct competition against other players: the reviewer explicitly describes battling other players in real-time combat. No other review text describes a dark-pattern mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the thrill of battling other players in real-time combat",
      "rule_applied": "definition: The game makes players compete against other players.",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes real-time combat between the player and other players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a progression barrier: at level 113, the game provides no extra energy, and continuing to make progress requires a purchase. The absence of extra energy alone does not establish a wait timer or daily schedule, so it does not support Playing by Appointment. No specific currency, purchase prompt, repeated task, or chance mechanic is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "to have any change at progressing you need to purchase.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall: the game reaches a point where further progress is contingent on making a payment.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly states that purchasing is necessary to continue progressing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an energy system that limits continued progress: after the player runs out of energy, they must wait for a long timer before energy is fully restored. This is an in-game regeneration timer that makes the player follow the game's schedule. The review does not describe paying to bypass the wait, so Pay to Progress is not supported. The comments about limited content and requested console features, storyline, maps, and customization are evaluations or hypothetical additions rather than current mechanics to label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "once you run out you can't really progress any further, and the timer is fairly long to recover fully",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that make players wait, including energy or lives refilling over time.",
      "rationale": "Running out of energy halts meaningful progress until a lengthy real-time recovery timer expires."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes failed Pokémon captures consuming Poké Balls quickly enough that the player says they must purchase more to continue capturing, which supports Pay to Progress. It also alleges that capture outcomes became unusually unfavorable after purchasing balls and that this depletion serves to drive further purchases; this is a claimed manipulation of outcome determination rather than a mere statement of bad luck, supporting Illusion of Control. The edit denies authorship of the review and the claimed conversations with other players, implying that a store review was posted under the player's identity, which supports Friend Spam / Impersonation. No external search was needed because Poké Balls and the capture mechanic are identifiable from the review and game name.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Narrative Obligation",
      "rationale": "Failed captures consume the usable Poké Balls, and the reviewer says continued capturing requires buying more."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "since I started purchasing, items \"poke\" balls that when I tried to make a capture that they keep breaking out so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The reviewer attributes repeated capture failures to a purchase-linked outcome pattern that drains balls and drives further purchases, implying manipulated capture odds."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FriendSpamImpersonation",
      "span": "I DID NOT WRITE THIS REVIEW.....I DIDN'T TALK TO SEVERAL PLAYERS BECAUSE I DO NOT KNOW ANYONE",
      "rule_applied": "Destination of the message",
      "rationale": "The edit denies authorship of a store review and the social claims it contains, implying that the review was posted under the player's identity."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes two current game behaviors: hard levels can take about a week to pass, and some tasks are described as impossible unless the player buys boosters. The week-long difficulty is slow progress without a named repetitive activity, timer, or required schedule, so it does not independently support Grinding or Playing by Appointment. Buying boosters is presented as the alternative that makes otherwise impossible tasks achievable, which supports Pay to Progress. The threatened uninstall is a future response, not a separate mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / Free alternative",
      "rationale": "The span makes purchasing boosters the route to completing tasks that are otherwise impossible, linking payment to in-game progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a time-limited event with a 525-dice reward. The player was close to completing it, ran out of dice, saw that two days remained, and then found the event changed after sleeping and logging in. This makes the opportunity depend on the game's schedule rather than the player's preferred timing. No explicit payment requirement, chance-based reward delivery, daily-login reward, repetitive task, or social mechanic is described. The event change is not explicitly attributed to a bug.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "I had 3 left to go and I ran out of dice, it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed.",
      "rule_applied": "Playing by Appointment; where a reward or event expires or changes on the game's clock and the span describes the resulting loss of opportunity, code T_PlayingByAppointment. FOMO is not added because anticipatory pressure to continue or return is not explicitly described.",
      "rationale": "The event's schedule controls when the player can obtain the nearly completed reward, and the event changes despite the displayed remaining time."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a referral mechanic that promises rewards for inviting friends to join Facebook, which is Social Pyramid Scheme and therefore Forced Fellowship. It also describes spending a large amount of in-game dice on a maximum bet for chance-based outcomes such as Shut Downs and Bank Heists; the stake is in-game currency, so Gambling applies even though real-money purchase is not stated. The repeated duplicate stickers describe unwanted or repeated drops from a variable reward system, supporting Reward Mania, but not Complete the Collection because the reviewer never says they are trying to finish a collection. The inability to log in is explicitly attributed to an internet connection error, so it is a technical fault and does not trigger a dark-pattern label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme indicator: rewards or incentives for inviting friends to join the game.",
      "rationale": "The review identifies an invitation-based reward mechanic that uses recruitment of new players as the incentive."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "when I finally had more than 500 dices and played max bet, absolutely NO LUCK! No Shut downs and no bank heists.",
      "rule_applied": "Gambling definition and boundary: an in-game stake or wager using earned currency in a chance-based activity qualifies even when no real-money purchase is described.",
      "rationale": "The player stakes dice in a maximum bet and describes chance-dependent outcomes that failed to occur."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way...",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards indicator: repeated or unwanted duplicate drops from a randomized reward pool.",
      "rationale": "Repeated duplicate stickers describe an unpredictable reward-delivery system that sustains attempts despite producing unwanted results."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an intentional interface behavior: when the game opens or the player enters 1v1, the UI automatically chooses the highest-stakes table rather than a neutral or lowest-stakes option. This directs the player toward a costly in-game choice and can cause accidental loss of coins. The reviewer further states that losing those coins forces them to buy more with real money, describing both a real-money-to-virtual-currency conversion and a payment barrier to continued play. The 1v1 reference also identifies player-versus-player competition. No web search was needed because all relevant mechanics are clear from the review.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins",
      "rule_applied": "Exciting UI Effect / UI Misdirection boundary: the interface directs the player toward a selected, high-cost option rather than presenting a neutral choice.",
      "rationale": "The UI automatically steers the player to the highest-bet table, creating a misleading default that can cause accidental selection."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency / boundary rule: the currency is the object acquired through a real-money purchase, even though the money amount is not stated.",
      "rationale": "The review describes in-game coins being depleted and replaced by purchasing more coins with real money."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / Pay to Progress: payment is portrayed as necessary after the player loses the coins needed to keep playing at the tables.",
      "rationale": "The wording that players are 'forced to buy' after losing their coins implies that spending money is required to continue playing."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "1v1 section",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "A 1v1 section is an explicit player-versus-player competitive mode."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes two mechanics. First, the game's random outcomes are perceived as systematically unfavorable, with the RNG described as being 'against you'; this is an allegation that the game manipulates or rigs outcome determination, fitting Illusion of Control. Second, later levels require a large in-game cost while returning barely enough to cover it, and the reviewer explicitly contrasts this with spending actual money. That implies the free route does not sustain smooth progression and payment is the alternative, fitting Pay to Progress. The review does not explicitly describe real money being converted into a named in-game currency, so Intermediate Currency is not assigned. The near break-even reward also does not establish a concrete leftover or shortfall for Waste Aversion, and no reward-delivery mechanism is described for Reward Mania.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's not rewarding playing the game without spending actual money.. at around level 100 it costs 2200 but if you win the level you'll barely make that back",
      "rule_applied": "Free alternative boundary under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The free route yields barely enough currency to cover the level cost, while spending actual money is presented as the alternative for continued progression."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the rng is so obviously against you",
      "rule_applied": "Illusion of Control definition and vs Alleged rigging boundary",
      "rationale": "The reviewer attributes unfavorable outcomes to the game's RNG being systematically biased against the player, alleging manipulated outcome determination."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an event-based competitive mechanic in which winning is allegedly tied to very large monetary spending rather than skill. The clearest evidence is the statement that the reviewer spent 30k to win the last event, alongside the explicit claims of pay to play and pay to win. This supports Pay to Progress because spending money provides the advantage needed to win, and Competition because the game contains events with winners. The phrase \"Same old same old grinder\" is only a general evaluation of repetitiveness and does not name a repetitive task required for progress, so Grinding is not assigned. No web search was needed.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Zero skill pay to win. Calculated total spent on last event was 30k to win.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress: patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money.",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly links spending 30k with winning the event, describing money as the route to an in-game advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "no opportunity for a general player to win an event",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: patterns that make players against each other in competition.",
      "rationale": "The reference to winning an event describes an event-based competitive outcome."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several mechanics, not merely dissatisfaction. It explicitly says spending money gives a competitive advantage, so this is Pay to Win. \"The update times for your base\" describes construction or upgrade timers that make the player wait. \"Everything scales endlessly, there is kinda no point to look forward to\" describes open-ended progression whose goal continually recedes, supporting Infinite Treadmill; however, \"just more grind\" does not name a repeated task, so it is not enough for Grinding on its own. The UI is said to be deliberately confusing and overwhelming in order to push purchases, which is UI Misdirection. The alliance passage describes hostile player-versus-player attacks against those outside an alliance, supporting Competition. It does not establish Forced Fellowship because the codebook excludes coerced grouping without recruitment or an obligation to avoid letting group members down, and it does not establish Encourages Anti-Social Behavior because no reward or advantage for the attacker is stated. No search is needed.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's set up in a pay to win way",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes a design in which paying players receive an advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play low-level indicator: in-game timers impose arbitrary waits.",
      "rationale": "Base upgrades are governed by timers that delay continued progression."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly, there is kinda no point to look forward to",
      "rule_applied": "Definition and boundary versus high level number or long play history: endless scaling and a receding or unattainable endpoint.",
      "rationale": "The progression target continually expands or recedes, leaving no meaningful completion state."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "The UI is deliberately confusing and overwhelming to achieve the effect that you need to buy stuff",
      "rule_applied": "UI Misdirection boundary versus UI complaint: an interface problem is explicitly attributed to steering players toward purchases.",
      "rationale": "The confusing and overwhelming interface is described as intentionally serving the purchase option against the player's interests."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "If you are not in one then you get attacked and bullied",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: the game makes players oppose one another in competition; ordinary hostile player-versus-player action codes Competition rather than anti-social behavior without a stated aggressor gain.",
      "rationale": "Players outside alliances are subjected to attacks by other players, establishing adversarial player competition."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a daily-reward system that cannot be completed unless the player places a 50,000-unit bet, so the daily reward is an active recurring incentive with a completion condition. In the context of a casino slots game, the phrase \"bet 50,000\" also describes wagering in a chance-based mechanic using in-game currency. The 40,000 timed-reward amount is a shortfall relative to the required bet, but it is not an item price or a demonstrated leftover purchase balance, so Waste Aversion does not apply. There is no described real-money purchase, progression gate, or wait timer.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I can't get my daily rewards unless I bet 50,000 with out it I can't complete my daily rewards",
      "rule_applied": "Daily Rewards definition and indicator: daily reward systems with completion requirements; the Playing by Appointment boundary does not apply because this is a standalone reward rather than a real-time gate on overall progression.",
      "rationale": "The game offers daily rewards but requires the player to complete a recurring daily condition to obtain them."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "unless I bet 50,000",
      "rule_applied": "Gambling indicator and Gambling vs Reward Mania boundary: a stated bet is a wager in a chance-based casino game, and gambling can be coded when the stake is in-game currency even without a real-money purchase being described.",
      "rationale": "The player must wager 50,000 in-game units, implying participation in a chance-based betting mechanic."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes success as financially gated: winning anything is said to require hundreds of dollars, and obtaining the final one or two stickers requires paying a large amount. The stickers are therefore part of a collection the player is expected to complete, while the apparent scarcity of the final items is tied directly to purchase. The statement that a goal may be completed only one time in a hundred expresses low success odds, but it does not identify a chance-based reward mechanic or allege rigging, so it does not independently support Gambling, Reward Mania, or Illusion of Control. The forced five-star review condition is not covered by any listed label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "if you want to win anything on this game it will cost hundreds of dollars.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win / Pay Wall definition: patterns that require players to spend money to progress or obtain success in-game.",
      "rationale": "The review states that winning anything is contingent on spending hundreds of dollars."
    },
